    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense for the primary fields. I'm also thinking about the 'description' or 'subject' fields for these edge cases. Sometimes the maps depict things that are hard to classify—like strange, unexplained phenomena or figures that don't fit into standard topographic or botanical taxonomies. If I'm using a controlled vocabulary, how do I handle entries that are fundamentally 'unidentifiable' or seem to defy the established categories in the thesaurus?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When your maps depict phenomena that defy your controlled vocabulary, you should treat those entries as a special category of \"unclassified material.\" You can use a reserved term within your thesaurus, perhaps something like \"Anomaly,\" \"Uncategorized Feature,\" or \"Phenomenon Undefined.\" If your thesaurus allows for free-text qualifiers or notes associated with a subject heading, use those to briefly describe the *nature* of the unexplained element without attempting a formal classification. This way, you maintain the structure of your controlled vocabulary while still flagging the unusual content.",
      "source": "target",
